    content: "# MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT: PIC TO PDF \342\200\224 NATIVE ANDROID DOCUMENT SCANNER AND PDF CREATOR\r\n\r\n## 1. PROJECT OVERVIEW\r\n\r\nAct as a senior Android developer, Kotlin engineer, Material Design 3 UI/UX designer, document-scanning specialist, and software architect.\r\n\r\nBuild a complete, functional, production-quality native Android application named **Pic To PDF**.\r\n\r\nThe application allows users to capture documents using their phone\'s camera, import existing images, arrange and edit the images, and convert them into professional PDF documents that can be saved, previewed, renamed, shared, and managed directly on the device.\r\n\r\nThis must be a real Android application, not a website, PWA, WebView application, React Native app, Flutter application, or demonstration prototype.\r\n\r\n### Mandatory technology stack\r\n\r\n- Native Android development.\r\n- Kotlin as the primary programming language.\r\n- Jetpack Compose for the entire application UI.\r\n- Material Design 3 (M3) for the design system and components.\r\n- Material 3 adaptive layouts where appropriate.\r\n- AndroidX Activity Result APIs for system activities.\r\n- Android\'s native camera capabilities.\r\n- Android Photo Picker for importing existing images.\r\n- Android `android.graphics.pdf.PdfDocument` for PDF generation.\r\n- Android Storage Access Framework (SAF) for saving documents to user-selected locations.\r\n- Kotlin Coroutines and Dispatchers.IO for background processing.\r\n- ViewModel and StateFlow for UI state management.\r\n- Room database for lightweight local document metadata and history, if needed.\r\n- DataStore for user preferences.\r\n- Coil for displaying local image thumbnails, if needed.\r\n- Gradle Kotlin DSL for build configuration.\r\n\r\nUse stable, mutually compatible dependencies wherever possible. Do not introduce experimental libraries unless absolutely necessary.\r\n\r\nDo not use Python, a remote PDF conversion API, Firebase, a cloud database, a server, or an external subscription-based service. All essential functionality must work locally on the device.\r\n\r\nThe application must work without an internet connection.\r\n\r\n## 2. APP IDENTITY AND BRANDING\r\n\r\nApplication name: Pic To PDF\r\n\r\nSuggested package name: `com.picpdf.creator`\r\n\r\nUse a polished, modern, professional visual identity appropriate for a productivity application.\r\n\r\nDesign a custom app icon featuring a document outline, a photograph symbol, and a PDF indicator. Use clean geometry and a recognizable silhouette that remains legible at small sizes.\r\n\r\nCreate a consistent visual language across the launcher icon, splash screen, onboarding, home screen, editor, PDF preview, settings, and dialogs.\r\n\r\n### Visual direction\r\n\r\n- Material Design 3.\r\n- Modern, minimal, elegant, and intuitive.\r\n- Rounded cards and buttons with consistent corner radii.\r\n- Proper visual hierarchy and generous spacing.\r\n- Clear typography and accessible contrast.\r\n- Material Symbols or suitable Material icons.\r\n- Subtle animations and tactile interaction feedback.\r\n- A carefully selected primary color with neutral surfaces.\r\n- Dynamic color support on compatible Android versions.\r\n- Light theme and dark theme.\r\n- System theme as the default theme setting.\r\n- Edge-to-edge layouts that respect system bars and display cutouts.\r\n\r\nDo not create a cluttered interface. Avoid excessive gradients, oversized decorative graphics, unnecessary dashboard statistics, or too many competing colors.\r\n\r\nUse actual functional controls instead of decorative mockups.\r\n\r\n## 3. APPLICATION ARCHITECTURE\r\n\r\nUse a maintainable architecture that separates the UI, business logic, data access, and Android platform integrations.\r\n\r\nSuggested structure:\r\n\r\n- `app/`\r\n  - `data/`\r\n    - `local/`\r\n    - `repository/`\r\n    - `preferences/`\r\n  - `domain/`\r\n    - `model/`\r\n    - `usecase/`\r\n  - `camera/`\r\n  - `image/`\r\n    - `processing/`\r\n    - `editing/`\r\n  - `pdf/`\r\n    - `PdfGenerator.kt`\r\n    - `PdfPageRenderer.kt`\r\n    - `PdfSettings.kt`\r\n  - `storage/`\r\n  - `navigation/`\r\n  - `ui/`\r\n    - `theme/`\r\n    - `components/`\r\n    - `screens/`\r\n      - `HomeScreen.kt`\r\n      - `CameraScreen.kt`\r\n      - `ImageImportScreen.kt`\r\n      - `PageEditorScreen.kt`\r\n      - `PdfSettingsScreen.kt`\r\n      - `PdfPreviewScreen.kt`\r\n      - `DocumentsScreen.kt`\r\n      - `SettingsScreen.kt`\r\n      - `AboutScreen.kt`\r\n  - `viewmodel/`\r\n  - `util/`\r\n\r\nThis structure is a guide, not a requirement to create unnecessary files. Keep the implementation understandable and avoid overengineering.\r\n\r\nUse a single-activity architecture with Jetpack Compose navigation.\r\n\r\nCreate reusable components for buttons, document cards, image thumbnails, page counters, empty states, progress indicators, confirmation dialogs, and error messages.\r\n\r\nKeep image processing and PDF generation out of composable functions. Composables must not perform expensive operations directly.\r\n\r\n## 4. SPLASH SCREEN AND FIRST LAUNCH\r\n\r\nCreate a brief, polished native splash screen using the Android SplashScreen API where supported.\r\n\r\nDisplay the Pic To PDF icon and application name using the app\'s visual identity.\r\n\r\nTransition smoothly into the home screen.\r\n\r\nDo not add a fake loading screen, arbitrary long delay, or unnecessary loading animation before the actual app starts.\r\n\r\nOn first launch, provide a short, skippable introduction explaining:\r\n\r\n1. Capture documents with the camera.\r\n2. Import existing photos.\r\n3. Arrange and enhance pages.\r\n4. Create and share PDFs offline.\r\n\r\nProvide a clear Start button and a Skip option.\r\n\r\nPersist onboarding completion so returning users go directly to the home screen.\r\n\r\n## 5. HOME SCREEN\r\n\r\nBuild a polished home screen that makes the main task immediately obvious.\r\n\r\n### Top app bar\r\n\r\nDisplay:\r\n\r\n- Pic To PDF branding.\r\n- A settings icon.\r\n- A suitable action for accessing the document library.\r\n\r\nUse appropriate Material 3 app bar components.\r\n\r\n### Primary action area\r\n\r\nDisplay two prominent actions:\r\n\r\n1. **Scan with Camera**\r\n2. **Import Photos**\r\n\r\nThe camera action should be the most prominent.\r\n\r\nUse large, accessible cards or buttons with recognizable icons and concise labels.\r\n\r\n### Recent documents\r\n\r\nDisplay recently created PDFs in a clean list or responsive grid.\r\n\r\nEach document item should show:\r\n\r\n- PDF document icon or generated thumbnail.\r\n- Filename.\r\n- Number of pages.\r\n- File size when available.\r\n- Creation date.\r\n- Overflow menu.\r\n\r\nOverflow actions must include appropriate options such as:\r\n\r\n- Open or preview.\r\n- Rename.\r\n- Share.\r\n- View file details.\r\n- Delete from the app\'s history.\r\n- Delete the actual file only when the app has permission and the user explicitly confirms.\r\n\r\nUse an empty state when no PDFs exist, with a clear invitation to create the first document.\r\n\r\nProvide a View All action that opens the complete document library.\r\n\r\n### Important\r\n\r\nDo not display fake document counts, sample files, fake recent activity, or hardcoded file sizes.\r\n\r\nShow real information obtained from locally available data.\r\n\r\n## 6. NATIVE CAMERA INTEGRATION\r\n\r\nImplement a working document-capture workflow using the phone\'s camera.\r\n\r\nUse the most appropriate Android-native approach.\r\n\r\nPreferred implementation: CameraX with a native camera preview embedded in a Jetpack Compose screen, using a compatible AndroidX CameraX version.\r\n\r\nIf a reliable embedded camera implementation cannot be completed, use the Android system camera through an appropriate Activity Result contract as a fallback. Do not substitute a fake camera screen.\r\n\r\n### Camera screen\r\n\r\nDisplay:\r\n\r\n- Live camera preview.\r\n- Capture button.\r\n- Flash toggle when supported.\r\n- Camera switching when supported.\r\n- Close or back action.\r\n- Image count.\r\n- Gallery import shortcut.\r\n- Guidance to position the document within the frame.\r\n\r\nSupport portrait and landscape orientations.\r\n\r\nRequest camera permission only when the user starts a camera-related action. If permission is denied, explain the situation and provide a clear alternative to import an existing image.\r\n\r\nHandle devices without a usable camera gracefully.\r\n\r\n### Capture behavior\r\n\r\nWhen the user taps Capture:\r\n\r\n1. Capture an actual photograph.\r\n2. Save the original capture to a suitable app-private temporary or working location.\r\n3. Add the captured image to the current document session.\r\n4. Display a thumbnail of the captured page.\r\n5. Allow additional captures without leaving the workflow.\r\n6. Provide a way to finish scanning and proceed to page editing.\r\n\r\nUse unique temporary filenames and prevent accidental overwriting.\r\n\r\nPreserve sufficient image resolution for readable documents, while avoiding unnecessary memory consumption.\r\n\r\nDo not keep multiple full-resolution copies of every image in memory.\r\n\r\n### Multi-page scanning\r\n\r\nThe user must be able to capture multiple pages in one session.\r\n\r\nFor example, a user can photograph five pages of a contract, arrange them, and export all five images as pages of a single PDF.\r\n\r\nAllow the user to review the pages before conversion.\r\n\r\n## 7. IMPORT EXISTING PHOTOS\r\n\r\nImplement Android\'s Photo Picker using the appropriate Activity Result API.\r\n\r\nAllow the user to select multiple images in one operation, subject to practical device and memory limits.\r\n\r\nSupport common image formats that Android can decode, including JPEG, PNG, and WebP where supported.\r\n\r\nHandle selected content URIs safely.\r\n\r\nThe app must not assume that imported files have permanent filesystem paths.\r\n\r\nIf imported URIs are needed after the picker closes, either process them promptly or retain the required access appropriately. Copy images into app-private working storage when necessary for a stable editing workflow.\r\n\r\nDisplay imported images as ordered page thumbnails.\r\n\r\nAllow additional images to be added to an existing session.\r\n\r\nAvoid requesting broad photo-library or storage permissions when the system picker and Storage Access Framework can fulfill the requirement.\r\n\r\nHandle unsupported formats, corrupted images, inaccessible files, and cancelled selection gracefully.\r\n\r\n## 8. PAGE EDITOR AND IMAGE MANAGEMENT\r\n\r\nCreate a dedicated editing screen after the user captures or imports images.\r\n\r\nThe screen should contain:\r\n\r\n- A main image preview.\r\n- A horizontal or vertical strip of page thumbnails.\r\n- A visible page count.\r\n- An Add Page action.\r\n- An Edit Image action.\r\n- A Reorder Pages action.\r\n- A Delete Page action.\r\n- A Continue to PDF Settings action.\r\n\r\n### Page ordering\r\n\r\nAllow users to change page order through intuitive drag-and-drop interactions.\r\n\r\nThe PDF must use the current displayed order, not the original selection order.\r\n\r\nProvide page numbers on thumbnails.\r\n\r\n### Page operations\r\n\r\nImplement:\r\n\r\n- Rotate left by 90 degrees.\r\n- Rotate right by 90 degrees.\r\n- Crop.\r\n- Reset edits.\r\n- Delete a page.\r\n- Duplicate a page if practical.\r\n- Reorder pages.\r\n- Add more images.\r\n\r\nRequire confirmation before destructive operations when appropriate.\r\n\r\nIf all pages are removed, show an empty state and prevent PDF generation until at least one valid page is added.\r\n\r\n### Cropping\r\n\r\nImplement a functional crop editor with a visible crop rectangle, adjustable edges or corners, and Apply and Cancel actions.\r\n\r\nUse a reliable image-processing implementation. Store crop parameters or generate a processed derivative without destroying the original image.\r\n\r\n### Perspective correction\r\n\r\nIf feasible, add document-edge detection and perspective correction to straighten photographed documents.\r\n\r\nTreat automatic edge detection as an enhancement, not a mandatory dependency. A manual crop tool must remain available even if automatic detection fails.\r\n\r\nDo not pretend to have detected document edges when no actual detection algorithm exists.\r\n\r\n### Image orientation\r\n\r\nRespect EXIF orientation when decoding imported photographs.\r\n\r\nEnsure that rotated images display correctly in the editor and in the final PDF.\r\n\r\nAvoid applying the same rotation twice.\r\n\r\n## 9. IMAGE ENHANCEMENT\r\n\r\nOffer a simple image enhancement panel designed for document readability.\r\n\r\nInclude the following options:\r\n\r\n- Original Color.\r\n- Grayscale.\r\n- Black and White.\r\n- Brightness adjustment.\r\n- Contrast adjustment.\r\n- Image rotation.\r\n\r\nOptional advanced enhancements:\r\n\r\n- Adaptive thresholding for document text.\r\n- Sharpening.\r\n- Shadow reduction.\r\n- Background cleanup.\r\n\r\nImplement actual pixel-processing operations, not just changes to the preview.\r\n\r\nUse a sensible processing pipeline so filters are applied consistently in the preview and exported PDF.\r\n\r\nKeep the original source image recoverable until the user finishes the workflow.\r\n\r\n### Processing modes\r\n\r\nOriginal Color:\r\nPreserve the original appearance as much as possible.\r\n\r\nGrayscale:\r\nConvert the image to grayscale while retaining readable details.\r\n\r\nBlack and White:\r\nApply a configurable threshold or suitable adaptive thresholding algorithm for high-contrast document scans.\r\n\r\nEnsure that small text remains readable. Avoid aggressive compression or thresholding that removes thin characters.\r\n\r\nIf an advanced enhancement cannot be implemented reliably, omit it rather than exposing a nonfunctional control.\r\n\r\n## 10. PDF GENERATION ENGINE\r\n\r\nThis is the core functionality of the application.\r\n\r\nImplement a real PDF-generation service using Kotlin and Android\'s native `android.graphics.pdf.PdfDocument` API.\r\n\r\nThe implementation must create valid, readable PDF files that can be opened by standard Android PDF viewers and other compatible PDF applications.\r\n\r\n### PDF creation workflow\r\n\r\nWhen the user confirms PDF creation:\r\n\r\n1. Validate the document session.\r\n2. Validate that every selected page has a readable image.\r\n3. Load and process each page using memory-efficient decoding.\r\n4. Apply rotation, crop, and image enhancements.\r\n5. Calculate the selected PDF page dimensions.\r\n6. Create a PDF page using `PdfDocument.PageInfo.Builder`.\r\n7. Draw the processed image onto the PDF page canvas.\r\n8. Preserve the image\'s aspect ratio unless the user explicitly selects an appropriate alternative.\r\n9. Finish the PDF page.\r\n10. Repeat for all pages in their current order.\r\n11. Write the completed document to the selected output stream.\r\n12. Close the PDF document and release resources in all success and failure paths.\r\n13. Validate the output and report the result.\r\n14. Present the completed PDF with Preview, Share, and Create Another actions.\r\n\r\nUse `try/finally` or equivalent structured resource management to ensure streams and PDF resources are closed properly.\r\n\r\nNever generate a PDF by simply renaming a JPEG file with a `.pdf` extension.\r\n\r\n### PDF page sizes\r\n\r\nOffer these paper-size options:\r\n\r\n- A4.\r\n- Letter.\r\n- Legal.\r\n- Original Image Size.\r\n- Fit to Page.\r\n\r\nUse standard dimensions with a clearly defined internal unit system. For standard paper sizes, use PDF page dimensions in points, with 72 points per inch.\r\n\r\nFor A4, use approximately 595.28 \303\227 841.89 points.\r\n\r\nFor Letter, use 612 \303\227 792 points.\r\n\r\nFor Legal, use 612 \303\227 1008 points.\r\n\r\nFor Original Image Size, calculate a sensible PDF page size based on image dimensions and a documented DPI assumption.\r\n\r\n### Page orientation\r\n\r\nSupport:\r\n\r\n- Portrait.\r\n- Landscape.\r\n- Auto.\r\n\r\nAuto orientation should choose the most suitable orientation for each page or apply a consistent document-wide orientation according to the selected setting.\r\n\r\nDo not stretch images disproportionately.\r\n\r\n### Image fitting\r\n\r\nOffer:\r\n\r\n- Fit to Page: fit the complete image within the page boundaries.\r\n- Fill Page: fill the page while preserving aspect ratio, cropping excess only when necessary.\r\n- Original Proportions: preserve the complete image with suitable margins.\r\n\r\nFor document scans, default to Fit to Page.\r\n\r\nAllow a configurable margin, with a sensible default such as 18\342\200\22324 points for standard paper sizes.\r\n\r\nKeep all image content within the printable PDF page area.\r\n\r\n### PDF quality\r\n\r\nOffer three practical quality presets:\r\n\r\n- Small File.\r\n- Balanced.\r\n- High Quality.\r\n\r\nSuggested behavior:\r\n\r\nSmall File:\r\n- Resize images to an appropriate resolution.\r\n- Apply stronger image compression where supported.\r\n- Prefer grayscale or monochrome output only when the user explicitly selects that processing mode.\r\n\r\nBalanced:\r\n- Preserve readable document text.\r\n- Use moderate image resizing and compression.\r\n- Optimize for everyday sharing.\r\n\r\nHigh Quality:\r\n- Preserve more original image detail.\r\n- Avoid unnecessary downscaling.\r\n- Accept larger output files.\r\n\r\nDo not promise a specific file size reduction. Actual results depend on the original images.\r\n\r\nUse a suitable JPEG encoding quality for processed photographic images. Recognize that Android\'s `PdfDocument` does not expose a general-purpose PDF compression-quality setting; image resizing and image encoding must be handled before drawing images into PDF pages.\r\n\r\nAvoid converting every image to a large uncompressed bitmap unnecessarily.\r\n\r\n### File naming\r\n\r\nAllow the user to name the output PDF.\r\n\r\nDefault to a meaningful name such as:\r\n\r\n`PicToPDF_2026-10-20_1530.pdf`\r\n\r\nUse a unique filename when a conflict exists.\r\n\r\nSanitize invalid filename characters.\r\n\r\nDo not silently overwrite existing documents.\r\n\r\n### Background processing\r\n\r\nPDF creation must run off the main thread.\r\n\r\nUse Kotlin Coroutines with `Dispatchers.IO` for decoding, processing, and writing.\r\n\r\nExpose generation progress through observable UI state.\r\n\r\nShow:\r\n\r\n- Current progress.\r\n- Number of pages processed.\r\n- Total number of pages.\r\n- A Cancel action where safe.\r\n\r\nAvoid freezing the UI during large conversions.\r\n\r\nHandle cancellation without leaving a corrupt output file marked as completed.\r\n\r\nWrite to a temporary output where practical and publish the final result only after successful completion.\r\n\r\n### Memory safety\r\n\r\nThe application must remain stable when processing several high-resolution images.\r\n\r\nImplement:\r\n\r\n- Downsampling where appropriate.\r\n- Efficient bitmap decoding.\r\n- Deterministic resource release.\r\n- Sequential page processing where possible.\r\n- Limits or warnings for exceptionally large batches.\r\n- Graceful handling of `OutOfMemoryError` where recovery is possible.\r\n\r\nDo not keep all full-resolution source images and processed copies in memory simultaneously.\r\n\r\nDo not catch fatal errors and then continue as if PDF generation succeeded.\r\n\r\n## 11. SAVE PDF FILES LOCALLY\r\n\r\nUse Android\'s Storage Access Framework, particularly `ACTION_CREATE_DOCUMENT` or an equivalent Activity Result contract, to let the user select where to save the PDF.\r\n\r\nUse the MIME type `application/pdf`.\r\n\r\nAllow users to save to:\r\n\r\n- A folder on the device.\r\n- A user-selected external storage location.\r\n- A supported document provider, including cloud-backed providers exposed by Android.\r\n\r\nCloud destinations are optional system-provided destinations, not a cloud backend implemented by this app.\r\n\r\nDo not require the user to grant unrestricted filesystem access.\r\n\r\nWhere appropriate, provide a default filename in the system save dialog.\r\n\r\nIf the user cancels the save dialog, return safely to the previous screen without losing the editable document session.\r\n\r\nAfter a successful save, display:\r\n\r\n- PDF filename.\r\n- Page count.\r\n- File size.\r\n- Save location when available.\r\n- Preview PDF.\r\n- Share PDF.\r\n- Return Home.\r\n\r\nOnly report success after the output has been written successfully.\r\n\r\n## 12. PDF PREVIEW AND SHARING\r\n\r\nImplement a useful PDF preview workflow.\r\n\r\nUse Android\'s native `PdfRenderer` for rendering saved PDF pages into previews, with appropriate resource management and background execution.\r\n\r\nDisplay pages in a vertically scrolling preview, with page navigation or a thumbnail strip where useful.\r\n\r\nProvide:\r\n\r\n- Zoom controls or pinch-to-zoom where feasible.\r\n- Page count.\r\n- Page navigation.\r\n- Share.\r\n- Save As.\r\n- Close.\r\n\r\nIf implementing a full PDF viewer becomes too complex, implement an accurate page preview and a working Open PDF action through an installed compatible viewer.\r\n\r\nDo not display a fake PDF preview using only the source images without confirming that the PDF was generated.\r\n\r\n### Sharing\r\n\r\nImplement sharing through Android\'s `ACTION_SEND` intent with:\r\n\r\n- MIME type `application/pdf`.\r\n- A valid content URI.\r\n- `FileProvider` when sharing an app-private file.\r\n- Temporary read permission for the receiving application.\r\n\r\nUse Android\'s Sharesheet.\r\n\r\nAllow the user to share the generated PDF through compatible installed apps.\r\n\r\nNever expose an unrestricted `file://` URI or a private absolute filesystem path to another application.\r\n\r\nIf sharing fails because no compatible application is available, display a helpful message.\r\n\r\n## 13. DOCUMENT LIBRARY AND HISTORY\r\n\r\nCreate a Documents screen for accessing PDFs created by the app.\r\n\r\nUse Room to store lightweight metadata if a local database is needed.\r\n\r\nSuggested metadata:\r\n\r\n- Internal document ID.\r\n- Display name.\r\n- App-managed file URI or other valid document reference.\r\n- Creation timestamp.\r\n- Last modified timestamp.\r\n- Page count.\r\n- File size.\r\n- Thumbnail reference, if maintained.\r\n\r\nDo not store the entire PDF or all image bitmaps in Room.\r\n\r\nSupport:\r\n\r\n- Recent documents.\r\n- All documents.\r\n- Search by filename.\r\n- Sorting by date or name.\r\n- Rename metadata or file when supported.\r\n- Preview.\r\n- Share.\r\n- Delete.\r\n- Empty and error states.\r\n\r\nDistinguish between an app-created file that can be managed and a user-selected external file that the app cannot modify.\r\n\r\nIf a user deletes a document from the library, explain whether the operation removes the actual file or only removes its entry from the app\'s history.\r\n\r\nDo not claim to manage every PDF on the device. The library should primarily represent files the app has created or has been explicitly granted access to.\r\n\r\nReconcile stale history entries when files are no longer accessible.\r\n\r\n## 14. SETTINGS\r\n\r\nBuild a clean settings screen containing:\r\n\r\n- Theme: System, Light, Dark.\r\n- Default paper size.\r\n- Default page orientation.\r\n- Default PDF quality.\r\n- Default image processing mode.\r\n- Default margin.\r\n- Remember last-used settings.\r\n- Clear temporary working files.\r\n- Privacy information.\r\n- About Pic To PDF.\r\n- App version.\r\n\r\nPersist preferences using DataStore.\r\n\r\nMake sure changing a default does not unexpectedly alter a document currently being edited.\r\n\r\nProvide confirmation for operations that could remove temporary work.\r\n\r\n## 15. ABOUT AND PRIVACY\r\n\r\nCreate an About screen with:\r\n\r\n- Pic To PDF.\r\n- App version.\r\n- A short app description.\r\n- Privacy information.\r\n- Open-source acknowledgments where applicable.\r\n\r\nPrivacy statement:\r\n\r\n\"Pic To PDF processes your images and creates PDF documents locally on your device. Your documents are not uploaded to a Pic To PDF server. No account or cloud database is required.\"\r\n\r\nEnsure this statement remains accurate: do not introduce analytics, advertising SDKs, telemetry, or remote processing that would contradict it.\r\n\r\nThe app must not request contacts, location, microphone, SMS, or phone permissions.\r\n\r\nRequest camera access only for camera functionality. Use the Android Photo Picker for selected images and SAF for file operations.\r\n\r\nDo not add internet permission unless an explicitly approved feature genuinely requires it.\r\n\r\n## 16. ERROR HANDLING AND USER FEEDBACK\r\n\r\nImplement clear, actionable feedback for:\r\n\r\n- Camera permission denied.\r\n- Camera unavailable.\r\n- Capture failure.\r\n- Image import failure.\r\n- Unsupported or corrupted image.\r\n- Empty document.\r\n- Crop or image-processing failure.\r\n- PDF generation failure.\r\n- Insufficient storage.\r\n- Save cancellation.\r\n- Save failure.\r\n- File already exists.\r\n- PDF preview failure.\r\n- Missing sharing application.\r\n- Inaccessible or deleted document.\r\n\r\nUse Material 3 Snackbar, AlertDialog, inline error states, and progress indicators appropriately.\r\n\r\nDo not show raw stack traces, internal exception messages, or technical jargon to ordinary users.\r\n\r\nLog useful diagnostic information in development builds without logging private document contents.\r\n\r\nEnsure progress indicators disappear when operations finish or fail.\r\n\r\nPrevent duplicate submissions if the user taps Generate PDF repeatedly.\r\n\r\n## 17. ACCESSIBILITY AND RESPONSIVENESS\r\n\r\nSupport:\r\n\r\n- Small Android phones.\r\n- Large phones.\r\n- Tablets.\r\n- Portrait and landscape.\r\n- Edge-to-edge layouts.\r\n- System font scaling.\r\n- TalkBack.\r\n- Accessible content descriptions.\r\n- Adequate touch target sizes.\r\n- High-contrast readable text.\r\n- Light and dark themes.\r\n\r\nUse responsive Compose layouts rather than fixed screen dimensions.\r\n\r\nEnsure bottom action buttons are not obscured by system navigation bars or display cutouts.\r\n\r\nAvoid clipping the last item in scrolling lists.\r\n\r\nUse stable keys in lazy lists.\r\n\r\nHandle configuration changes and process recreation without losing important document metadata. Preserve temporary session data when practical, and clearly handle unrecoverable temporary files.\r\n\r\n## 18. PERFORMANCE AND RELIABILITY\r\n\r\nOptimize the application for everyday Android devices, including devices with limited memory.\r\n\r\nAvoid unnecessary background services.\r\n\r\nDo not keep the camera active after leaving the camera screen.\r\n\r\nRelease CameraX resources and image-processing resources correctly.\r\n\r\nAvoid blocking the main thread.\r\n\r\nPrevent accidental duplicate page insertion.\r\n\r\nEnsure rotations, crops, filters, and page ordering are reflected consistently in the generated PDF.\r\n\r\nUse predictable temporary-file cleanup.\r\n\r\nNever delete source images imported from the user\'s gallery.\r\n\r\nDo not delete a saved PDF without explicit user confirmation.\r\n\r\nMake sure a failed conversion does not leave a broken file in the completed-document library.\r\n\r\n## 19. TESTING REQUIREMENTS\r\n\r\nCreate meaningful unit tests for core business logic.\r\n\r\nTest:\r\n\r\n- PDF page-size calculations.\r\n- Aspect-ratio preservation.\r\n- Page ordering.\r\n- Rotation and crop transformations.\r\n- Image-filter behavior.\r\n- Filename sanitization.\r\n- PDF generation with one image.\r\n- PDF generation with multiple images.\r\n- Empty-input validation.\r\n- Corrupted-image handling.\r\n- Duplicate filenames.\r\n- Save cancellation.\r\n- Temporary-file cleanup.\r\n- Error-state handling.\r\n\r\nAdd instrumentation or UI tests for the main navigation and document-creation workflow where practical.\r\n\r\nVerify that a generated PDF can be reopened and that its page count matches the expected number of pages.\r\n\r\nVerify that generated pages are not blank, rotated incorrectly, or clipped.\r\n\r\nTest on at least one emulator and, when available, a physical Android device.\r\n\r\n## 20. BUILD AND PROJECT CONFIGURATION\r\n\r\nConfigure a complete Android Studio project using Kotlin and Jetpack Compose.\r\n\r\nProvide:\r\n\r\n- Root Gradle configuration.\r\n- App-level Gradle configuration.\r\n- Version catalog if used.\r\n- Android manifest.\r\n- Required resources.\r\n- Theme definitions.\r\n- Launcher icon resources.\r\n- Compose navigation.\r\n- Room database configuration if used.\r\n- DataStore configuration if used.\r\n- CameraX configuration if used.\r\n- FileProvider configuration if used.\r\n- ProGuard or R8 rules where necessary.\r\n- Unit and instrumentation test setup.\r\n- README with build instructions.\r\n\r\nChoose a sensible `minSdk` compatible with the selected libraries and target a current stable Android SDK.\r\n\r\nUse stable compatible dependency versions and document any important version constraints.\r\n\r\nDo not use nonexistent dependency coordinates, deprecated APIs when a suitable supported alternative exists, or arbitrary versions copied from unrelated projects.\r\n\r\nThe project must sync and compile in Android Studio.\r\n\r\n## 21. MONETIZATION READINESS WITHOUT PAID DEPENDENCIES\r\n\r\nKeep the architecture ready for optional future monetization, but do not implement advertisements, subscriptions, payments, tracking, or account registration in the initial release.\r\n\r\nThe initial app must remain fully functional without purchasing anything.\r\n\r\nDo not integrate an ad SDK merely to make the project monetization-ready.\r\n\r\nDo not introduce any feature that requires a paid API, cloud service, subscription, or mandatory account.\r\n\r\nIf future monetization is considered, it must not compromise local document privacy or prevent users from accessing PDFs they have already created.\r\n\r\n## 22. DEFINITION OF DONE\r\n\r\nThe project is complete only when the following end-to-end workflow works:\r\n\r\n1. The user launches Pic To PDF.\r\n2. The user taps Scan with Camera.\r\n3. The app opens a real camera preview and captures a document page.\r\n4. The user captures additional pages or imports existing images.\r\n5. The user sees the actual page thumbnails.\r\n6. The user rotates, crops, filters, deletes, or reorders pages.\r\n7. The user chooses PDF page size, orientation, margins, and quality.\r\n8. The user enters a filename.\r\n9. The app generates a valid multi-page PDF locally.\r\n10. The user saves the PDF to a chosen location.\r\n11. The app confirms success only after the save completes.\r\n12. The user can preview the generated PDF.\r\n13. The user can share the PDF through Android\'s Sharesheet.\r\n14. The document appears in the app\'s library when it is managed by the app.\r\n15. The user can reopen the app later and access previously saved documents when their files remain accessible.\r\n16. The complete workflow functions without internet access.\r\n\r\nEvery primary action must perform its advertised operation.\r\n\r\nNo fake camera preview, fake conversion, placeholder PDF, mock save operation, hardcoded success message, or decorative-only control is acceptable.\r\n\r\n## 23. IMPLEMENTATION INSTRUCTIONS FOR GOOGLE AI STUDIO\r\n\r\nBuild the actual application, not just a design proposal or a written tutorial.\r\n\r\nStart by establishing the project structure and implementing the essential end-to-end workflow.\r\n\r\nPrioritize functionality in this order:\r\n\r\nPhase 1:\r\n- Native Android project setup.\r\n- Material 3 theme and navigation.\r\n- Home screen.\r\n- Camera capture.\r\n- Photo import.\r\n- Multi-page editing and reordering.\r\n- Native PDF generation.\r\n- Save PDF using SAF.\r\n\r\nPhase 2:\r\n- Crop and rotation.\r\n- Grayscale and black-and-white processing.\r\n- Paper size, orientation, and quality settings.\r\n- PDF preview and sharing.\r\n- Document library and history.\r\n\r\nPhase 3:\r\n- Advanced document enhancement.\r\n- Improved thumbnails and search.\r\n- Dark theme refinement.\r\n- Accessibility improvements.\r\n- Tests, performance optimization, and final polish.\r\n\r\nDo not stop after generating the home screen or creating a static UI prototype.\r\n\r\nDo not omit critical source files or replace the implementation with pseudocode.\r\n\r\nIf the environment cannot generate every file in a single response, work in coherent stages while maintaining a consistent project structure. Clearly identify which files have been implemented and which remain incomplete.\r\n\r\nAfter implementation, review the code for compilation errors, missing imports, incompatible dependencies, incorrect permissions, invalid URI handling, resource leaks, and nonfunctional controls.\r\n\r\nRun available build and test commands. Fix the errors that can be reproduced before declaring the project complete.\r\n\r\nDeliver a fully functional native Android Pic To PDF application with a polished Material 3 interface, reliable local PDF creation, and no mandatory paid services or cloud dependencies."
    num_lines: 905
    num_bytes: 31498
    line_range_bytes: 31498
  }
}
